Answer fdinfo of a sus_kstat path with the mount id and inode of the rule

Upstream rewrites the mnt_id and ino lines of /proc/<pid>/fdinfo/N for an app's fd on a sus_kstat
path, in fs/proc/fd.c's seq_show(). stat() already answers from the rule, and without this an app
could read the two side by side and see the real file behind a path whose stat() lies.

A module cannot patch seq_show(), so the two numbers are replaced in the formatted text at its
return. The text has no device, so the rule is matched on the file itself, found the way seq_show()
finds it: the fdinfo inode names a task and a descriptor, and the descriptor table gives the
file. A probe cannot sleep, so the table is read under rcu and the task lock, which keeps
exit_files() from freeing it, without taking a reference. The match is on inode and device as in
upstream, so a bind mount of the path is answered as well, and the text's inode is checked against
the file's in case the descriptor was replaced in between.

Where upstream prints the rule's spoofed inode unconditionally, this prints it only when the rule
spoofs the inode, as stat() does; otherwise a rule without it would show inode 0 in fdinfo beside
the real one in stat(). The mount id is the one the path had when the rule was added.

seq_show has no table that holds its address and, above 5.15, no name unique to the file
reading it (four symbols named seq_show on 6.1 and 6.6), so every match is probed, up to 32, and the
handler tells its own file apart by the "fdinfo" directory its dentry sits under, the same approach
susfs_open_redirect.c already uses for one line of the same file. Armed with the first kstat rule,
removed at unload. Needs fs/proc/internal.h for struct proc_inode, reached like fs/mount.h through
the -I$(srctree)/fs the build already has.

